problems.solution:
**A model answer.** `tests/login-page.ts`:

```ts
import { type Page } from '@playwright/test';

export class LoginPage {
  readonly page: Page;

  constructor(page: Page) {
    this.page = page;
  }

  async signIn(email: string, password: string) {
    await this.page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
    await this.page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Email').fill(email);
    await this.page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Password').fill(password);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
  }

  async signedInName(name: string) {
    return await this.page.getByText(name).innerText();
  }
}
```

`tests/practice-page-object.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage } from './login-page';

test('the dashboard shows the name of the user who signed in', async ({ page }) => {
  const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
  await loginPage.signIn('leela@gmail.com', 'Leela@123');
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);

  const name = await loginPage.signedInName('Leela');
  expect(name).toBe('Leela');
});
```

**Why `login-page.ts` does not end in `.spec.ts`.** Playwright runs every file whose name ends in `.spec.ts` as a test file. The page object contains no tests, so it must not match that pattern.

**What to check in your own answer.** The test file contains no locators: they are all in `login-page.ts`. The page object returns the name, and the test makes the assertion. `expect(name).toBe('Leela')` has no `await`, because `name` is already a string, not the page, so there is nothing to wait for.
